"aimarket-mcp by alexar76 | Glama (https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/alexar76/aimarket-mcp)\nciteturn11view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/alexar76/aimarket-mcp\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 749\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Glama】\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Servers】【3†Connectors】【4†Tools】【5†Clients】【6†Inspector】【7†New Chat】[Button: Sign Up]\nL5: \nL6: 【1†Glama】\nL7: \nL8: 【8†MCP】\nL9: \nL10: 【2†Servers】\nL11: \nL12: [Input: Search MCP servers, tools, and more]\nL13: \nL14: 【9†Image: alexar76】\nL15: # aimarket-mcp\nL16: \nL17: Favorite\nL18: \nL19: 【10†Agent Orchestration】【11†Browser Automation】【12†Search】\nL20: \nL21: by 【13†alexar76】\nL22: \nL23: 【14†Overview】【15†Schema】【16†Source】【17†Related Servers】【18†Discussions】【19†Admin】\nL24: \nL25: 【20†Python】\nL26: \nL27: 【21†Remote】\nL28: \nL29: What can you do with this server?\nL30: \nL31: This MCP gateway exposes five hardened tools for web access, verification, and AIMarket capability discovery/invocation.\nL32: \nL33:   * `web_fetch_tool`: Fetch a public URL and return extracted main text, with SSRF protections and sanitized `<untrusted>` output.\nL34:   * `web_search_tool`: Run live DuckDuckGo searches and return top snippets, sanitized and marked untrusted.\nL35: \nL36:   * `metis_verify_tool`: Send a question/task to Metis cognition + verification, returning an answer plus `verify_score`/`verified` metadata and selectable route depth.\nL37: \nL38:   * `market_search_tool`: Discover priced AIMarket capabilities by intent, with optional category and max-price filters; free and wallet-free.\nL39:   * `market_invoke_tool`: Execute one discovered capability on the hub’s free trial tier and return output plus a signed-receipt nonce; reports 402 when trial allowance is exhausted.\nL40: \nL41: Which integrations are available for this server?\nL42: \nL43: Provides live web search via 【22†DuckDuckGo】, returning top snippets.\nL44: \nL45: How do I use aimarket-mcp?\nL46: \nL47:   1. Click on \"Deploy Server\".\nL48: \nL49:   2. Wait a few minutes for the server to deploy. Once ready, it will show a \"Started\" state.\nL50:   3. In the chat, type `@` followed by the MCP server name and your instructions, e.g., \"`@aimarket-mcp` search for recent breakthroughs in AI\"\nL51: \nL52: That's it! The server will respond to your query, and you can continue using it as needed.\nL53: \nL54: Here is a 【23†step-by-step guide with screenshots】.\nL55: > 🔄 Synced from a monorepo — but with a live history. `aimarket-mcp` mirrors the canonical AI-Factory monorepo. History here is append-only (no force-push). Pull requests are welcome — merged PRs are imported back into the monorepo and re-synced here, so your contribution becomes canonical. 💬 【24†Issues†github.com】 · 【25†Pull requests†github.com】 both welcome.\nL56: # aimarket-mcp — ecosystem MCP gateway\nL57: \nL58: > 🌐 English · 【26†Русский†github.com】 · 【27†Español†github.com】 · 【28†Français†github.com】 · 【29†中文†github.com】 · 【30†Glossary†github.com】\nL59: > ### 🟢 Just want to try the marketplace? Install nothing.\nL60: >\nL61: > Paste `https://modelmarket.dev/mcp` into your MCP client and you have `market_search` and `market_invoke` against the live hub, with a few free trial invokes per caller and a signed receipt for each — no wallet, no key, no install. → `【31†docs/hosted-mcp-endpoint.md†github.com】`\nL62: >\nL63: > Install this package when you want the other three tools (`web_fetch`, `web_search`, `metis_verify`), or want to point the market tools at a hub of your own.\nL64: One MCP gateway. Five hardened tools. Shared by Metis, ARGUS, and the ecosystem.\nL65: \nL66: Transport: stdio (`aimarket_mcp/stdio_server.py`) for Glama / Claude Desktop / Cursor, built with the official Model Context Protocol Python SDK (`【32†mcp†github.com】`, `FastMCP`). Also ships Streamable-HTTP on `:9090` for self-hosted deployments (`aimarket-mcp-http`, Docker Compose).\nL67: Item  | Location\nL68: MCP entrypoint (stdio)  | `aimarket_mcp/stdio_server.py`\nL69: MCP gateway (HTTP)  | `【33†aimarket_mcp/server.py†github.com】`\nL70: Tool handlers + security  | `【34†aimarket_mcp/tools.py†github.com】`, `【35†aimarket_mcp/security.py†github.com】`\nL71: Glama / Docker (stdio)  | `【36†Dockerfile†github.com】`, `【37†glama.json†github.com】`\nL72: Self-host HTTP  | `【38†Dockerfile.http†github.com】`, `【39†docker-compose.yml†github.com】`\nL73: Compatible hosts: Claude Desktop, Cursor, Glama, and any MCP client that supports stdio or Streamable-HTTP.\nL74: ## Tools\nL75: Tool  | What it does  | Hardening\nL76: `web_fetch`  | Fetch a URL, return main text (readability-lite)  | SSRF-guarded; output sanitized + `<untrusted>`-wrapped\nL77: `web_search`  | Live DuckDuckGo search → top snippets  | output sanitized + `<untrusted>`\nL78: `metis_verify`  | Metis cognition + verification envelope  | returns answer + `verify_score` / `verified` gate\nL79: `market_search`  | Discover priced capabilities on an AIMarket hub  | free; no wallet, key or channel; the hub lists only what it can execute\nL80: `market_invoke`  | Run one on the hub's free trial tier  | returns the signed receipt nonce; reports the hub's 402 verbatim when the allowance is spent\nL81: ### Example — buy a GAIA Open-Meteo reading\nL82: \nL83:     `market_search_tool(intent=\"gaia weather open-meteo\")\nL84:     market_invoke_tool(\nL85:       capability_id=\"gaia.weather.read@v1\",\nL86:       product_id=\"gaia.gateway\",\nL87:       source_hub=\"https://iot.modelmarket.dev\",\nL88:       # input JSON with device_id om-wx-01 — see tool schema / hub docs\nL89:     )`\nL90: Or the same HTTP the tool wraps:\nL91: \nL92:     `curl -s -X POST \"${AIMARKET_HUB_URL:-https://modelmarket.dev}/ai-market/v2/invoke\" \\\nL93:       -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\nL94:       -H 'X-AIMarket-Sandbox-Visitor: vis_mcp_om_wx' \\\nL95:       -d '{\"capability_id\":\"gaia.weather.read@v1\",\"product_id\":\"gaia.gateway\",\nL96:            \"source_hub\":\"https://iot.modelmarket.dev\",\"input\":{\"device_id\":\"om-wx-01\"}}'`\nL97: That is the easy path (trial/sandbox). Proving an external wallet paid on-chain is a separate escrow flow (`openChannel` → DebitAuthorization → hub debit/settle) — see `【40†docs/onchain-journal.md†github.com】` §3l–§3m and `【41†gaia/docs/LIVE-RELAYS.md†github.com】`.\nL98: \nL99: Why a gateway (not per-agent tools): generic capabilities are written once; the security core lives in one audited place. Ecosystem-specific capabilities live in their own MCP servers (`aimarket-oracle-gateway`, `aimarket-plugins`).\nL100: Related MCP server: 【42†mcp-web-search-server】\nL101: ## Configure (env)\nL102: var  | meaning\nL103: `AIMARKET_METIS_URL`  | Metis verify API base (default `https://metis.modelmarket.dev`)\nL104: `AIMARKET_METIS_KEY`  | optional bearer for Metis verify\nL105: `AIMARKET_SEARCH_URL`  | DuckDuckGo HTML endpoint override\nL106: `AIMARKET_HUB_URL`  | AIMarket hub for `market_search` / `market_invoke` (default `https://modelmarket.dev`)\nL107: `AIMARKET_API_KEY`  | Credit-account key from the hub's `/start` page (`https://modelmarket.dev/start`): priced `market_invoke` calls are paid from its prepaid balance; while it is short the free trial still applies. In HTTP mode it pays for every caller of that server\nL108: `AIMARKET_SANDBOX_VISITOR`  | Trial identity for `market_invoke`; random per install, set it to keep an allowance across reinstalls\nL109: `AIMARKET_MCP_KEY`  | HTTP only — bearer auth key\nL110: `AIMARKET_MCP_PRODUCTION`  | HTTP only — `1` requires `AIMARKET_MCP_KEY` (fail-closed)\nL111: `AIMARKET_MCP_RATE`  | HTTP only — requests/min per key/IP (default 120)\nL112: `AIMARKET_MCP_PORT`  | HTTP only — listen port (default 9090)\nL113: ## Run (stdio — Glama / Claude Desktop)\nL114: \nL115: Claude Desktop (`mcpServers` entry) — no clone, no working directory to get wrong:\nL116: \nL117:     `{\nL118:       \"mcpServers\": {\nL119:         \"aimarket-mcp\": {\nL120:           \"command\": \"uvx\",\nL121:           \"args\": [\"aimarket-mcp\"]\nL122:         }\nL123:       }\nL124:     }`\nL125: \nL126: `uvx` fetches the published package and runs its `aimarket-mcp` console script, which is the stdio server. With the package already installed, `\"command\": \"aimarket-mcp\"` and no args does the same thing.\nL127: From a checkout, for development:\nL128: \nL129:     `pip install -e .\nL130:     python -m aimarket_mcp.stdio_server`\nL131: ## Run (HTTP — self-host)\nL132: \nL133:     `pip install -e .\nL134:     AIMARKET_MCP_KEY=sk-... AIMARKET_MCP_PRODUCTION=1 aimarket-mcp-http   # :9090\nL135:     # or: docker compose up -d   (uses Dockerfile.http)`\nL136: \nL137: ### Cursor / Claude (Streamable HTTP)\nL138: \nL139:     `{\nL140:       \"mcpServers\": {\nL141:         \"aimarket-web\": {\nL142:           \"type\": \"streamable-http\",\nL143:           \"url\": \"http://127.0.0.1:9090/mcp\",\nL144:           \"headers\": {\nL145:             \"Authorization\": \"Bearer YOUR_AIMARKET_MCP_KEY\"\nL146:           }\nL147:         }\nL148:       }\nL149:     }`\nL150: ## Publish on Glama\nL151: \nL152: Listing: 【14†aimarket-mcp】. Glama generates a container from the build settings in its admin panel:\nL153: \nL154: Field  | Value\nL155: Build steps  | `[\"bash scripts/glama_install.sh\"]`\nL156: CMD arguments  | `[\"mcp-proxy\", \"--\", \".venv/bin/python\", \"-m\", \"aimarket_mcp.stdio_server\"]`\nL157: Placeholder parameters  | `{}`\nL158: Pinned commit SHA  | empty (latest synced commit)\nL159: Build, inspect, and publish a release after syncing source changes. A repository sync alone does not refresh the deployed tool catalog or its quality evaluation. The catalog should contain all five tools listed above. See 【43†Glama build and release instructions†github.com】.\nL160: ## Consumers\nL161: \nL162:   * Connected successfully? Share your host, configuration, and one working tool using the 【44†Show and tell template†github.com】.\nL163: \nL164:   * Metis — enable the preset:\nL165: \nL166:     `enable_mcp_tools: true\nL167:     mcp_ecosystem_presets: [aimarket-web]`\nL168: \nL169:   * ARGUS — add the server to `argus.config.json` `mcpServers` (see that repo).\nL170: \nL171: ## Test\nL172: \nL173:     `pip install -e '.[dev]' && pytest -q`\nL174: ## Registries\nL175: \nL176: See the 【45†directory submission checklist and current status†github.com】 before submitting or refreshing a listing.\nL177: \nL178: Registry  | Listing\nL179: Glama  | 【14†glama.ai/mcp/servers/alexar76/aimarket-mcp】\nL180: Official MCP Registry  | `io.github.alexar76/aimarket-mcp` — `server.json` + GitHub Actions\nL181: PyPI  | `pip install aimarket-mcp`\nL182: GitHub Releases  | 【46†github.com/alexar76/aimarket-mcp/releases†github.com】\nL183: ## Available Tools\nL184: \nL185: 5 tools\nL186: \nL187: 【47†market_invoke_tool】A\nL188: \nL189: Run one capability from market_search on the hub's free trial tier.\nL190: Returns the capability's output plus the nonce of the hub-signed receipt, so the result is verifiable against the hub's published key rather than trusted on faith. A few trial invokes are granted per installation; after that the hub answers 402 and this reports that instead of fabricating a result. Paid access needs an on-chain escrow deposit, which is an operator action and deliberately not available here.\nL191: \nL192: Parameters JSON Schema\nL193: Name  | Required  | Description  | Default\nL194: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL195: `input`  | No  | Input object for the capability; omit or {} when it takes none.  |\nL196: `product_id`  | No  | The product_id from market_search, e.g. 'prod-skopos'. Inferred from the capability_id prefix when empty, which is often wrong — pass it.  |\nL197: `source_hub`  | No  | The source_hub from market_search, when it showed one. Required for federated capabilities — most of the catalogue; without it the hub looks for the capability locally and answers 404.  |\nL198: `capability_id`  | Yes  | Exact capability_id from market_search, e.g. 'skopos.fleet.status@v1'.  |\nL199: #### Output Schema\nL200: \nL201: Parameters JSON Schema\nL202: \nL203: Name  | Required  | Description\nL204: --- | --- | ---\nL205: `result`  | Yes  |\nL206: #### 【48†TDQS†tdqs.dev】\nL207: \nL208: A 4.3/5.0\nL209: \nL210: Behavior 4/5\nL211: \nL212: Does the description disclose side effects, auth requirements, rate limits, or destructive behavior?\nL213: No annotations are provided, so the description carries the full behavioral burden and does so well: it discloses the trial quota per installation, that exhaustion returns 402 and is reported rather than faked, and that results come with a hub-signed receipt nonce verifiable against the published key. It does not disclose possible side effects of the invoked capability, which is the remaining gap.\nL214: Agents need to know what a tool does to the world before calling it. Descriptions should go beyond structured annotations to explain consequences.\nL215: \nL216: Conciseness 5/5\nL217: \nL218: Is the description appropriately sized, front-loaded, and free of redundancy?\nL219: \nL220: Four sentences, front-loaded with the core action, and every sentence earns its place: trial tier, verifiability, quota/402 behavior, paid-access exclusion. No filler or repetition of the schema.\nL221: Shorter descriptions cost fewer tokens and are easier for agents to parse. Every sentence should earn its place.\nL222: \nL223: Completeness 5/5\nL224: \nL225: Given the tool's complexity, does the description cover enough for an agent to succeed on first attempt?\nL226: \nL227: With an output schema present, return values need not be spelled out, and the description covers the operational essentials an agent needs (quota limits, error semantics, verifiability, paid-path exclusion) alongside fully documented parameters.\nL228: Complex tools with many parameters or behaviors need more documentation. Simple tools need less. This dimension scales expectations accordingly.\nL229: \nL230: Parameters 3/5\nL231: \nL232: Does the description clarify parameter syntax, constraints, interactions, or defaults beyond what the schema provides?\nL233: Schema description coverage is 100%, so parameters (including the non-obvious product_id and source_hub federation caveats) are already documented. The description adds no parameter-level guidance beyond the schema, so the baseline of 3 applies.\nL234: \nL235: Input schemas describe structure but not intent. Descriptions should explain non-obvious parameter relationships and valid value ranges.\nL236: \nL237: Purpose 5/5\nL238: \nL239: Does the description clearly state what the tool does and how it differs from similar tools?\nL240: States a specific verb+resource with scope: 'Run one capability from market_search on the hub's free trial tier.' It explicitly grounds the tool against the sibling market_search_tool (this invokes what that one discovers), so an agent can distinguish them without opening schemas.\nL241: \nL242: Agents choose between tools based on descriptions. A clear purpose with a specific verb and resource helps agents select the right tool.\nL243: \nL244: Usage Guidelines 4/5\nL245: Does the description explain when to use this tool, when not to, or what alternatives exist?\nL246: \nL247: Clear context: use after market_search, on the free trial tier, and a when-not (paid access requires on-chain escrow, deliberately unavailable here). It does not name an alternative tool to use for paid access, but the trial/no-trial boundary is well stated.\nL248: \nL249: Agents often have multiple tools that could apply. Explicit usage guidance like \"use X instead of Y when Z\" prevents misuse.\nL250: \nL251: 【49†market_search_tool】A\nL252: Discover paid AI capabilities listed on an AIMarket hub, with prices.\nL253: \nL254: Free — no wallet, key or payment channel. Returns capability_id, product_id, price per call in USD and a trust score; pass both ids to market_invoke to run one. The hub lists only capabilities it can actually execute, so an empty result means it genuinely does not sell that. Set AIMARKET_HUB_URL to point at a different hub.\nL255: \nL256: Parameters JSON Schema\nL257: Name  | Required  | Description  | Default\nL258: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL259: `limit`  | No  | Maximum results, capped at 25.  |\nL260: `intent`  | Yes  | What you want done, in plain language. Example: 'security posture of my servers' or 'verifiable random number'. Max 500 chars.  |\nL261: `category`  | No  | Optional category filter, e.g. 'security'. Empty for none.  |\nL262: `max_price_usd`  | No  | Optional cap on price per call in USD. 0 for no cap.  |\nL263: #### Output Schema\nL264: \nL265: Parameters JSON Schema\nL266: \nL267: Name  | Required  | Description\nL268: --- | --- | ---\nL269: `result`  | Yes  |\nL270: #### 【48†TDQS†tdqs.dev】\nL271: \nL272: A 4.3/5.0\nL273: \nL274: Behavior 4/5\nL275: \nL276: Does the description disclose side effects, auth requirements, rate limits, or destructive behavior?\nL277: With no annotations the description carries the full burden, and it does well: it discloses zero cost/auth requirements, the shape of returned metadata, the search-is-exhaustive semantics behind an empty result, and the AIMARKET_HUB_URL override for pointing at another hub. It leaves out ranking/ordering behaviour and what the trust score means, so it is strong but not exhaustive.\nL278: Agents need to know what a tool does to the world before calling it. Descriptions should go beyond structured annotations to explain consequences.\nL279: \nL280: Conciseness 5/5\nL281: \nL282: Is the description appropriately sized, front-loaded, and free of redundancy?\nL283: \nL284: Front-loaded with the core purpose, then caveats in descending order of importance (free, return shape, empty-result semantics, config override). Every sentence carries information and nothing is padded.\nL285: Shorter descriptions cost fewer tokens and are easier for agents to parse. Every sentence should earn its place.\nL286: \nL287: Completeness 5/5\nL288: \nL289: Given the tool's complexity, does the description cover enough for an agent to succeed on first attempt?\nL290: \nL291: An output schema exists, yet the description still gives the agent enough to act: what a call costs, what comes back, what an empty result means, and where the next call goes. Nothing needed to call or interpret it correctly is missing.\nL292: Complex tools with many parameters or behaviors need more documentation. Simple tools need less. This dimension scales expectations accordingly.\nL293: \nL294: Parameters 3/5\nL295: \nL296: Does the description clarify parameter syntax, constraints, interactions, or defaults beyond what the schema provides?\nL297: Schema description coverage is 100% (limit, intent, category, max_price_usd are all documented with defaults and examples), so the schema already does the work. The description adds no parameter-level detail such as how intent is matched or how max_price_usd interacts with the returned prices, making this baseline territory.\nL298: \nL299: Input schemas describe structure but not intent. Descriptions should explain non-obvious parameter relationships and valid value ranges.\nL300: \nL301: Purpose 5/5\nL302: Does the description clearly state what the tool does and how it differs from similar tools?\nL303: \nL304: Specific verb and resource ('Discover paid AI capabilities listed on an AIMarket hub, with prices') with the scope made explicit. It also names the downstream sibling ('pass both ids to market_invoke to run one'), which separates it cleanly from market_invoke_tool and the generic web_search_tool.\nL305: Agents choose between tools based on descriptions. A clear purpose with a specific verb and resource helps agents select the right tool.\nL306: \nL307: Usage Guidelines 4/5\nL308: \nL309: Does the description explain when to use this tool, when not to, or what alternatives exist?\nL310: Clear context for use: it is free, needs no wallet/key/payment channel, and the next step is stated. The empty-result rule ('the hub lists only capabilities it can actually execute, so an empty result means it genuinely does not sell that') is genuinely useful guidance. However, it never states when to prefer this over web_search_tool or metis_verify_tool, so no explicit alternatives or exclusions.\nL311: Agents often have multiple tools that could apply. Explicit usage guidance like \"use X instead of Y when Z\" prevents misuse.\nL312: \nL313: 【50†metis_verify_tool】A\nL314: \nL315: Run Metis cognition + verification on an input; returns answer plus verify_score/verified.\nL316: \nL317: Calls the Metis `/v1/verify` API. Response includes machine-readable metadata `[verify_score=… status=… route=… verified=…]` so agents can fail-closed when confidence is insufficient. Configure AIMARKET_METIS_URL and optional AIMARKET_METIS_KEY.\nL318: Parameters JSON Schema\nL319: \nL320: Name  | Required  | Description  | Default\nL321: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL322: `input`  | Yes  | Question or task for Metis to answer through its cognition + verification envelope. Example: 'Is 2+2=4?' or 'Summarize the MIT license in one sentence.'  |\nL323: `route`  | No  | Metis cognition depth: fast (single pass), thinking (deeper), council (multi-agent, default), agent (tool-using). Higher routes cost more latency but improve verify_score.  | council\nL324: #### Output Schema\nL325: \nL326: Parameters JSON Schema\nL327: \nL328: Name  | Required  | Description\nL329: --- | --- | ---\nL330: `result`  | Yes  |\nL331: #### 【48†TDQS†tdqs.dev】\nL332: \nL333: A 4.3/5.0\nL334: \nL335: Behavior 4/5\nL336: \nL337: Does the description disclose side effects, auth requirements, rate limits, or destructive behavior?\nL338: \nL339: No annotations, so description carries burden. It explains the response includes machine-readable metadata and fail-closed behavior. Could add more on error handling.\nL340: \nL341: Agents need to know what a tool does to the world before calling it. Descriptions should go beyond structured annotations to explain consequences.\nL342: \nL343: Conciseness 5/5\nL344: Is the description appropriately sized, front-loaded, and free of redundancy?\nL345: \nL346: Three short sentences, front-loaded with purpose, efficient.\nL347: \nL348: Shorter descriptions cost fewer tokens and are easier for agents to parse. Every sentence should earn its place.\nL349: \nL350: Completeness 5/5\nL351: \nL352: Given the tool's complexity, does the description cover enough for an agent to succeed on first attempt?\nL353: \nL354: Given schema and output schema, description adds configuration and behavioral notes. No major gaps.\nL355: Complex tools with many parameters or behaviors need more documentation. Simple tools need less. This dimension scales expectations accordingly.\nL356: \nL357: Parameters 3/5\nL358: \nL359: Does the description clarify parameter syntax, constraints, interactions, or defaults beyond what the schema provides?\nL360: \nL361: Schema coverage is 100% so baseline is 3. Description adds little beyond schema: mentions route enum options but schema already enumerates them.\nL362: Input schemas describe structure but not intent. Descriptions should explain non-obvious parameter relationships and valid value ranges.\nL363: \nL364: Purpose 5/5\nL365: \nL366: Does the description clearly state what the tool does and how it differs from similar tools?\nL367: \nL368: Description clearly states it runs Metis cognition and verification, returns answer plus verify_score/verified, and names the API endpoint. Siblings are web tools, so no confusion.\nL369: Agents choose between tools based on descriptions. A clear purpose with a specific verb and resource helps agents select the right tool.\nL370: \nL371: Usage Guidelines 4/5\nL372: \nL373: Does the description explain when to use this tool, when not to, or what alternatives exist?\nL374: \nL375: Describes configuration and that it calls a specific API, but does not explicitly state when to use or not use versus alternatives. However, context is clear enough.\nL376: Agents often have multiple tools that could apply. Explicit usage guidance like \"use X instead of Y when Z\" prevents misuse.\nL377: \nL378: 【51†web_fetch_tool】A\nL379: \nL380: Fetch a web page by URL and return its main text content.\nL381: \nL382: SSRF-hardened (scheme allow-list, private-IP block, per-redirect re-validation, size cap). Output is sanitized, role-marker stripped, and wrapped in `<untrusted>…</untrusted>` before it can reach a model — safe for agent consumption with explicit untrusted marking.\nL383: \nL384: Parameters JSON Schema\nL385: Name  | Required  | Description  | Default\nL386: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL387: `url`  | Yes  | Public http(s) URL to fetch. Private IPs, localhost, and non-http schemes are rejected (SSRF guard). Example: https://example.com/docs/guide  |\nL388: `max_chars`  | No  | Maximum characters of extracted main text to return (default 20000).  |\nL389: #### Output Schema\nL390: \nL391: Parameters JSON Schema\nL392: \nL393: Name  | Required  | Description\nL394: --- | --- | ---\nL395: `result`  | Yes  |\nL396: #### 【48†TDQS†tdqs.dev】\nL397: \nL398: A 4.2/5.0\nL399: \nL400: Behavior 4/5\nL401: \nL402: Does the description disclose side effects, auth requirements, rate limits, or destructive behavior?\nL403: \nL404: Despite no annotations, the description provides substantial behavioral details: SSRF hardening (scheme allow-list, private-IP block, per-redirect re-validation), size cap, output sanitization, role-marker stripping, and untrusted wrapping. This goes beyond the basic fetch operation.\nL405: Agents need to know what a tool does to the world before calling it. Descriptions should go beyond structured annotations to explain consequences.\nL406: \nL407: Conciseness 5/5\nL408: \nL409: Is the description appropriately sized, 